#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221129 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "99f2f8a2-f5aa-5a32-b29f-8e1f79f504cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21692" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1, \\, 2]^T.$ What is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/2c994a3f48f4d9e12a179b03538977f72e033c5e539ec6f73c6d4529c1674299.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/d1aac4dbbbd1d7579a0a03c1440b3140efc18f43d6a8016e867e9419af693675.png)\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/6118cf06e6accf9f29665d66c4c4d00e0e7e2eb8bdbd98bd9044ee418422ca59.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/fb0d2631e167027b68263b62af5ba65d91442fdc89d1c16e39d6299f97835307.png)\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "e61881ec-4361-554d-b374-35b8b3f64366" :question/math-academy-id "e-21693" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0, \\, -2]^T.$ What system is obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \n= \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,\n$$\n\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/951509e719a40caba7ecc5872d983b6cf83128d77af670c88846d2a06d329d8e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/a3d02714f52ce42ec3e6d78d9cdd91f4c2ca14a59948f078d93318c94ec5e31a.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069931/assets/cce8b4b07839490b3be0841f8a4e6e73b69426426504625cedecf738947b046d.png)\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t) = u(t), \\qquad\ny(t) = v(t) - 2, \\qquad\nx'(t) = u'(t), \\qquad\nv'(t) = y'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+(v(t)-2)+2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =5x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)+10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =5u(t)-(v(t)-2)^{2}+3(v(t)-2)+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)+4)+3v(t)-6+10 \\\\  & =5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+7v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/id #uuid "9215f93a-14ad-5be5-9f27-25ba556252e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21694" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=3x-1$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?\n\n- $y=3x+3$\n\n- $y=-x$\n\n- $y=3x$\n\n- $y=-3x$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=3x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4588a11-9267-5bd3-97c0-df80640bb03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364475} {:answer/id #uuid "e174f83c-c3d7-5fa2-846a-437aa96125eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-3v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364476} {:answer/id #uuid "c7353098-4ac1-5508-be4a-929a95837a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364477} {:answer/id #uuid "b5830ab2-699b-53bf-b98e-2cdfb6aa850c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-3{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364478} {:answer/id #uuid "e70b268c-fee7-584c-a5a4-696f0ad31e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3{u}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4588a11-9267-5bd3-97c0-df80640bb03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364475} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8ba004f-2e21-56b7-82b5-70b22d40c7a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a3c0b53-eab9-5b58-92da-6bae03210acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333671" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}(t)-2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+2)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1-2u(t)-2+v(t)+2-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+4(v(t)+2)-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-{v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)-4+4v(t)+8-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4296219e-6e3d-5b72-9d74-55c5e1801055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}-{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364482} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e15ba5-d7f4-545e-990a-40d4cf85497f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364483} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c21628-6c25-50a4-aa86-2323e98849a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364484} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8459dc-570f-5a69-917c-239023a47cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364485} {:answer/id #uuid "5606a964-87de-5574-b471-d69242c7e2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1e15ba5-d7f4-545e-990a-40d4cf85497f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364483} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4e7885-dea3-5caa-be8f-f91f213b7a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "331c7839-3f32-5d34-bd91-dc6188dfcb86" :question/math-academy-id "q-333682" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $A?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${v}_{1}$ or ${v}_{2}$ is the vector $-{v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f738e6-1fb4-50ef-bba1-09ba7a648fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220448} {:answer/id #uuid "f633c220-ef07-55cd-a8fe-4fecf9903200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220449} {:answer/id #uuid "1133f9ec-29d6-5b33-bb14-ce70b1fe75c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220450} {:answer/id #uuid "2876d161-685b-561d-8c20-e82d1bcc43d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220451} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd4197-451c-59c1-827e-4e9b80259440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f738e6-1fb4-50ef-bba1-09ba7a648fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220448} :answer-field/id #uuid "53c6b181-ec24-5a98-bbac-4b80559e73f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc805b5f-d7a6-53b7-9121-090baedc8eb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333683" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=2x-3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e8143d1-62d6-5533-9e21-282e4071cccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364489} {:answer/id #uuid "1e15e928-0a5d-5289-8a22-f7f263438642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364490} {:answer/id #uuid "2f17a6cb-8cba-5c19-a763-fafaae98a450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364491} {:answer/id #uuid "ec13fc6b-2566-51c7-aa36-8b702b9d6b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364492} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdba148-7431-50d3-afcf-a699cdd44139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8143d1-62d6-5533-9e21-282e4071cccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364489} :answer-field/id #uuid "028335b8-6d92-5ecf-91fa-1f32c431139e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f439789e-cdab-5342-8d21-9a3ce1e3d364" :question/math-academy-id "q-335073" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb69658-745e-5351-b689-5a5cee1a83c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364496} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4c5aeb-cce8-549c-847f-5ed4e539340e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364497} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e73b11-d675-558f-8da1-01027085bcb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364498} {:answer/id #uuid "1acf55d8-57a6-5043-9ce8-a1270510b072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364499} {:answer/id #uuid "b15b9e36-afe7-5769-90b3-e6f164d4ef0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e73b11-d675-558f-8da1-01027085bcb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364498} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8debf3-5e9e-53f0-92a3-048fa2f7f0bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cd87a99-e6c2-5b74-be33-b608333e4e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-335079" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739ca367-6dc8-5f21-bd75-0cb90375c1f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t)-1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364503} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3906ee-3ab9-559a-815b-f0505e130ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364504} {:answer/id #uuid "029b8c47-ff2a-569b-9948-76e2b4305751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364505} {:answer/id #uuid "f7533251-0e92-5a0f-b6d1-97ec22af0f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364506} {:answer/id #uuid "8b239660-ea2f-57ef-8308-1db194c55adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029b8c47-ff2a-569b-9948-76e2b4305751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364505} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cc2c1f-6ccb-5b67-83ba-b364765306c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c89cb551-d1e6-5832-a074-6afc9561ced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335207" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daad85e0-d252-5e46-b314-bb0bf8f4aa5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t)+4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)+9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364510} {:answer/id #uuid "38c83fd3-b49d-5c65-87b9-efaae9517320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364511} {:answer/id #uuid "90d55b8b-5ae4-53cf-9459-316b8cdb95a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364512} {:answer/id #uuid "265879b9-a4ba-534b-b65d-81d959c3ca67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364513} {:answer/id #uuid "286638f0-ecd3-51bf-b84d-8c8b0db4e9b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "265879b9-a4ba-534b-b65d-81d959c3ca67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364513} :answer-field/id #uuid "41cbc6ff-2e9a-534c-826d-cd46e55f9095" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc6d171a-592a-572e-86fe-d9a714207d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-335212" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)+4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+4y(t)+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,-2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa4f5a7f-8008-5a23-a62e-54ceae2b1fe0" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfae2756-bebf-5a94-9565-53164f718823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=5v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364517} {:answer/id #uuid "35ae5acc-f0e7-5763-b9ea-ee3190bbb38a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t)+5 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)-9\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364518} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4d0af5-449f-5017-ac3c-441273db7eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t)+1 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364519} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8c241c-004f-5771-826a-6d831d71bdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4ac630-af78-5e27-9532-4fd52cee3768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t)+2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b8c241c-004f-5771-826a-6d831d71bdd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364520} :answer-field/id #uuid "9425db89-bdf8-5171-9caa-91f8d8a00051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "745434e5-21ff-571f-b4ba-3dac76316242" :question/math-academy-id "q-335213" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)+5 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+5y(t)-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[2,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5301ce5c-5f44-50f9-a62d-0eb5d4a59ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364524} {:answer/id #uuid "4be061e3-eee9-5d37-b538-efe82fcd99b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364525} {:answer/id #uuid "4587dacc-b60d-5373-82e7-0fc9fc817ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364526} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfab591-dd48-5163-985f-369bfebc5aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364527} {:answer/id #uuid "13d836c5-6bcc-54c1-9b8f-5f3a7899ecb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be061e3-eee9-5d37-b538-efe82fcd99b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364525} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfaa1055-44a7-52d2-9117-2a111fc46aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9934bc5f-ba47-56cd-bf7b-e37132886f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335371" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1+v(t)+1-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+3(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+3v(t)+3-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9188e0ec-e72e-5afd-b816-fff0084e3a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364531} {:answer/id #uuid "d3af0b59-09f6-5c61-8c22-8c062e45d157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364532} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc03e38-f0ae-5637-9454-a4b493c9c081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364533} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d894a1-c838-5f3a-a491-1c93ebcf87ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364534} {:answer/id #uuid "bec9d762-104b-5b4b-aaca-6c310c7e082f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8d894a1-c838-5f3a-a491-1c93ebcf87ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364534} :answer-field/id #uuid "376b025c-b442-5883-8af3-db664c1161c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce9aec9d-c2f9-599b-b8da-3b93db4c1e9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335376" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2u(t)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+3(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+3v(t)+6-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b6e477-ee48-59cc-984f-c7d8564f93d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-2{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364538} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8d7b1e-f9f7-5cbd-8331-b5fd992f97a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364539} {:answer/id #uuid "844b645b-fa5f-50c8-95ca-0b1af1175158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364540} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d559-dc25-5ef2-8407-480e9c115e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364541} {:answer/id #uuid "659bd375-d3e1-5d3e-b3c9-7305b116c4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=u(t)+2{u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d559-dc25-5ef2-8407-480e9c115e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364541} :answer-field/id #uuid "c49f71d1-d62a-54ed-b247-f377b497850b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77fea41-127f-5d8e-ade7-a3fd6550869a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335384" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c6e6984-7029-5e3e-bfcc-b8e553658911" :knowledge-point/title "Shifting the Equilibrium to the Origin: Non-Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af503d8b-c15e-5e59-ac0d-212d448386ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2{u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-2{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364545} {:answer/id #uuid "94da8e6a-7eda-5ff7-a693-d44f4d150fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364546} {:answer/id #uuid "c00de80c-b463-5e54-8f75-48bf7cb52405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4{u}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364547} {:answer/id #uuid "9910db5c-b93e-58bb-bb04-1af03685b359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+2{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364548} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b679d3-ab73-5fc6-9c11-d05704140bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4b679d3-ab73-5fc6-9c11-d05704140bc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364549} :answer-field/id #uuid "928af578-3572-5ce4-9e81-766db94481bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83e2b1e4-9301-5667-8010-f374b0cce368" :question/math-academy-id "q-335478" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[3,\\,0]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ef1febd-81e8-5e85-8ce9-5666189b95bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364552} {:answer/id #uuid "36d2c8e7-fb38-5710-9784-fb51a749512a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364553} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0cc743-a71b-5074-95fe-e1a71aa572bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1}+{x}^{∗}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364554} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a0d2c1-8583-55b2-9a6e-89fc4adfb7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364555} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbbda0e-89da-50bd-9963-33a22970afd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}-‖{x}^{∗}‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36d2c8e7-fb38-5710-9784-fb51a749512a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364553} :answer-field/id #uuid "75b67485-8ee0-5eac-85e2-6956e0322acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbd0072c-9617-5dfd-8c07-1e1b8ec76bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335485" :question/problem "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[-2,\\,4]^{T}.$ The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvalue of the matrix $A?$" :question/worked-solution "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvalues as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ or any expressions that are equal to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only quantity that is guaranteed to be an eigenvalue of $A$ is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5242b2ad-aa36-55a6-ac51-4ec80c5c8167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An asymptotically stable spiral sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364559} {:answer/id #uuid "70d95d0a-568d-58b6-a808-a631be1b12a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364560} {:answer/id #uuid "4cdb3b52-ea8a-5384-a8dd-76cb29898495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stable star node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364561} {:answer/id #uuid "98c5756c-98ee-5bd7-a5a3-dfb973306080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An unstable spiral source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364562} {:answer/id #uuid "33d11bfe-8c0e-53a6-bfa4-e507ca31c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stable center" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cdb3b52-ea8a-5384-a8dd-76cb29898495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A stable star node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364561} :answer-field/id #uuid "3608012c-898f-503e-ab42-7599fb0d61b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91e2e185-1f83-5298-a5cd-e56df6733f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335516" :question/problem "A system of ODEs has a stable star node at ${x}^{∗}.$ The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following best describes the point $(0,0)$ for the shifted system?" :question/worked-solution "Shifting does not alter the local behavior near an equilibrium. So, the equilibrium retains its original classification (saddle, node, spiral, center, etc.).\n\nRecall that the shift moves the equilibrium to the origin.\n\nThus, the equilibrium at $(0,0)$ in the shifted system has the same classification as ${x}^{∗}$ in the original system.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"A stable star node\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd72f8f-010f-58c8-97b5-420df1cbb6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Shifted System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5893b10d-0693-59dc-b07d-aded06049c28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly three equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364566} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf9d773-2597-5fae-af12-a252b19ab3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has infinitely many equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364567} {:answer/id #uuid "f84c27dd-70a2-5194-9779-2ddab0978500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364568} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d7e1d6-6eeb-5df8-a0c3-713c4026d553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has exactly one equilibrium point." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364569} {:answer/id #uuid "3a457e23-cbef-51f8-b844-325e516ebf5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system has no equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84c27dd-70a2-5194-9779-2ddab0978500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af3a157-9b35-531b-9c6b-a06be7623083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5f8ef0c-e30b-50dd-a0ab-1a422595b973" :question/math-academy-id "q-335522" :question/problem "A nonlinear system of ODEs has exactly two equilibrium points. The system is then shifted using the transformation $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ where ${x}^{∗}$ is one of the equilibria. Which of the following statements about the number of equilibria in the shifted system is guaranteed to be true?" :question/worked-solution "A shift of coordinates is a translation of the phase space and does not alter the structure of the system’s equilibrium set. Each equilibrium point of the original system is translated to a corresponding equilibrium in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, although one of the equilibria is moved to the origin, the total number of equilibrium points remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"The shifted system still has exactly two equilibrium points.\""}]}
